HOW THE OBLIGATION TRAVELS, and what is still owed.

  DONE 2026-07-30: `tools/release.ps1` copies NOTICE, LICENSE, TRADEMARKS.md and
  ALL FIVE font licence texts into the release directory, and refuses to ship if
  any of them is missing. Until then `dist/` held four executables and a checksum
  file and nothing else, so every copy distributed from it was out of step with
  three licences this file had correctly recorded -- OFL clause 2, the Bitstream
  Vera notice reached through Hack, and MIT for emoji-icon-font. The record was
  complete and the package was not. The Plex faces did not create that gap; they
  took the count of unaccompanied OFL faces from one to three, which is what made
  anyone look.

  AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THIS FIXED ONE OF THE THREE AND SAID IT HAD FIXED ALL
  OF THEM, which is worth recording because it is the same failure one level up.
  That version copied `IBMPlex-OFL.txt` only, so the package still carried no MIT
  permission notice and no Bitstream Vera permission notice -- the two the
  script's own comment named -- while this paragraph read DONE in the past tense.
  A five-line list that covers two of six faces looks exactly like a five-line
  list that covers all of them. What made the difference on the second pass was
  running `tools/release.ps1` and reading the file listing, rather than reading
  the list in the script.

  STILL OWED: `pl licences` prints the compiled-in feature-database attribution
  and does not print this font block. Note that it is `pl.exe`, which embeds no
  fonts at all -- the six faces are in `polylinker.exe`, which has no licence
  view of its own. So the honest statement of the gap is that the GUI cannot yet
  state its own font attribution from inside itself, and it should be able to.
  Two more OFL faces make that worse rather than better.

