    WHY REJECTED, in order of weight:
      1. Its cmap covers space, hyphen and ALL 26 lowercase letters, every one
         of them with an hmtx advance of ZERO, and it carries a single `liga`
         feature holding 1,512 ligature rules. Three of those rules are spelled
         entirely from the DNA alphabet: a+t, c+a+t and t+a+g. `Molecule::seq`
         is case-preserved, so lowercase sequence is a real input. With Phosphor
         ahead of the text face in the Monospace chain a 60-base lowercase row
         measures 172.5 pt instead of 414.0 and "acgt" renders at zero width --
         the column-to-base map drifts and every click lands on the wrong base.
         It is safe only by chain ordering, with nothing asserting the ordering.
      2. The icon audit found exactly one control in the whole application where
         an icon says something a word does not: the three toolbar menus, which
         look identical to buttons. That is a triangle. A triangle is three
         points, and `main::menu_with_caret` paints them -- no font, no bytes,
         no cmap, no licence entry, no tofu.
      3. `accesskit` is enabled. Phosphor's glyphs live in the Private Use
         Area, so any control whose visible text is an icon exposes a PUA
         codepoint, or nothing, as its accessible name.
      4. 488,636 bytes for one triangle -- 5.63% of the 8,682,496-byte binary
         it was measured against, 5.38% of the 9,082,368 the Plex faces left.
         The denominator is named because a bare percentage stops being true
         the next time the executable changes size.

